\description{
Fits a mixed-effects model: the random effects that \code{\link[=insert]{insert()}} with effect
formulas put into the parameter transformation are integrated out, and the
fixed parameters and the hyperparameters of the random effects are
estimated by maximizing the marginal likelihood.

A group, a level of the grouping of a random term such as a patient or a
cell line, may own several conditions, which share the random effects of the
group.
Normal, Laplace, spike-and-slab and Dirichlet-process random effects of one
grouping may be combined. Random terms over several groupings, nested or
crossed, are normal and integrated per connected component of the
conditions.

Methods:
\describe{
\item{\code{"focei"}}{Laplace approximation at the conditional modes with the
exact derivative of the volume term. The default, and the method for
Laplace, spike-and-slab and Dirichlet-process random effects (at most 14
groups) and for several groupings.}
\item{\code{"quadrature"}}{adaptive sparse-grid quadrature around the modes,
started from the FOCEI fit; section Quadrature.}
\item{\code{"laplaceEM"}}{ECM on the Laplace approximation with the
covariance-corrected update of \code{Omega}.}
\item{\code{"saem"}}{stochastic-approximation EM, also for Dirichlet-process
random effects; section SAEM.}
}
}
\section{Quadrature}{

Method \code{"quadrature"} alternates between placing the nodes around the
modes at the current parameters and one trust step over every parameter on
these frozen nodes. The nodes of the mu-referenced fixed effects are frozen
in \verb{phi = X beta + eta}. Entries of \code{control$quadrature}:
\describe{
\item{\code{tol}}{allowed change of -2 log L by the next level, default 0.1,
split over the groups; each group takes the lowest level from the current
stage of the group up within \code{tol / G} and never a lower one than before.
\code{Inf} keeps the
levels of the stages.}
\item{\code{levels}}{the stages, default \code{K + 1} with \code{K} the number of random
effects of a group; level \code{K + s} integrates polynomials of total
degree \verb{2 s + 1} exactly.}
\item{\code{maxLevel}}{highest level, default \code{K + 8}.}
\item{\code{tolDecrement}}{a stage ends when a Newton step on the rebuilt
nodes would lower -2 log L by less than this, default 1e-4, or than the
nodes resolve.}
\item{\code{maxEcmPerStage}, \code{cm1Control}, \code{maxCm1Iter}}{rebuilds per stage
(default 10), \code{\link[=trust]{trust()}} settings and iterations of a step (default 30).}
\item{\code{pruneTol}}{nodes whose weight lies this much in log below the
largest are dropped, default \code{Inf}.}
\item{\code{certificate}}{\code{FALSE}, or a list of \code{points} (default 256) and
\code{seed} (default 1): importance sampling at the estimate from a t5
proposal at the mode of each group. A group is flagged where the two
-2 log L differ by more than three standard errors plus the error the
level of the group allows, or the Pareto shape of the weights exceeds 0.7.}
\item{\code{epsEcm}, \code{maxEcm}}{for \code{"laplaceEM"}: the extrapolated distance to
the fixed point at which it stops, default 1e-5, and the iteration limit.}
}
}

\section{SAEM}{

Metropolis draws of the random effects by random-walk and independence
proposals at the conditional modes, Robbins-Monro averages of their
sufficient statistics, the mu-referenced fixed effects and \code{Omega} in
closed form, the other parameters by Newton steps on averaged quadratic
models. Entries of \code{control$saem}:
\describe{
\item{\code{nBurnin}, \code{nEM}}{at most that many burn-in and averaging
iterations, default 200 and 300.}
\item{\code{nMcmc}, \code{nLaplace}}{random-walk and independence steps per
iteration, default 3 each.}
\item{\code{laplaceEvery}, \code{laplaceScale}}{iterations between refreshes of the
modes, default 5, and the factor of the proposal covariance, default
1.5.}
\item{\code{window}, \code{mcTol}}{the burn-in ends when no parameter has a
significant slope over the last \code{window} iterations (default 25), the
averaging when the expected rise of -2 log L from the Monte Carlo error
is below \code{mcTol} (default 0.05).}
\item{\code{particles}}{with Dirichlet-process random effects the particles of
the sequential importance sampling of -2 log L, default 500.}
}
With Dirichlet-process random effects a Gibbs sweep draws the clusters of
the groups (Neal 2000, algorithm 8), the averaging runs \code{nEM} iterations,
and the reported clusters are the most frequent partition of the late
draws.
}

\section{Sparse random effects}{

With Laplace, spike-and-slab or Dirichlet-process random effects the
parameters and hyperparameters move in one trust region. The integral of a
group is exact where the data couple none of the factors and the sum over
the subsets of the spike-and-slab random effects of the group stays within a budget of
nodes; else it is the expectation consistent (EC) approximation, and a
trial point at which EC does not converge is a failed evaluation. Entries of \code{control$laplace}:
\describe{
\item{\code{maxOuter}}{iterations of the trust region, default 200.}
\item{\code{trustControl}}{the \code{\link[=trust]{trust()}} settings; \code{ftol}, \code{mtol} and \code{gtol}
of the \code{tolControl} there also end the search of the modes (else 1e-10).}
}
}

\examples{
\donttest{
## Theophylline, 12 subjects: one-compartment model with first-order absorption in closed
## form, log parameters with a normal random intercept per subject, additive error
th <- datasets::Theoph
th$Subject <- as.character(th$Subject)
subjects <- unique(th$Subject)
data <- as.datalist(data.frame(name = "y", time = th$Time, value = th$conc, sigma = NA_real_,
                               condition = th$Subject))
## second derivatives forward over reverse, the default of em()
dm <- c("forward", "reverse", "forward-reverse")
x <- Xt()
g <- Y(c(y = "amt * Ka / (V * Ka - Cl) * (exp(-Cl / V * time) - exp(-Ka * time))"),
       states = "time", modelname = "theoph_obs", compile = FALSE, derivMode = dm,
       outdir = tempdir())
err <- Y(c(y = "sigma_add"), g, attachInput = FALSE, modelname = "theoph_err", compile = FALSE,
         derivMode = dm, outdir = tempdir())
## one condition per subject with the subject dose; the effect formula replaces each log parameter
## by an intercept plus a random effect per subject
doses <- tapply(th$Dose * th$Wt, th$Subject, `[`, 1)[subjects]
trafo <- branch(define(NULL, "x <- x", x = c("Ka", "V", "Cl", "amt", "sigma_add")),
                table = data.frame(ID = subjects, dose = doses, row.names = subjects))
trafo <- insert(trafo, "amt <- dose", dose = dose)
trafo <- insert(trafo, "x <- exp(x)", x = c("Ka", "V", "Cl", "sigma_add"))
p <- P(insert(trafo, Ka + V + Cl ~ 1 + (1 | ID)), method = "explicit", compile = FALSE,
       modelname = "theoph_p", derivMode = dm, outdir = tempdir())
compile(g, err, p, output = tempfile("theoph"))
obj <- normL2(data, g * x * p, errmodel = err)
init <- c(Ka = 0, V = 3, Cl = 0.7, sigma_add = log(0.5))

## FOCEI fit, the estimates and the random effects per subject
fit <- em(obj, randomEffects(p), init = init)
fit
summary(fit)
fit$omega
head(fit$random)

## quadrature started from the FOCEI estimates, and SAEM
fitQ <- em(obj, randomEffects(p), init = fit, method = "quadrature", verbose = FALSE)
fitS <- em(obj, randomEffects(p), init = init, method = "saem", verbose = FALSE)
c(focei = fit$value, quadrature = fitQ$value, saem = fitS$value)
}
\dontrun{
## Ka and Cl with correlated random effects, V with a Laplace one: a second transformation
p2 <- P(insert(trafo, Ka + Cl ~ 1 + (1 | p | ID), V ~ 1 + (1 | gr(ID, dist = laplace()))),
        method = "explicit", compile = FALSE, modelname = "theoph_p2", derivMode = dm,
        outdir = tempdir())
compile(p2, output = tempfile("theoph"))
fit2 <- em(normL2(data, g * x * p2, errmodel = err), randomEffects(p2), init = init)
fit2$omega
fit2$strengths
AIC(fit, fit2)
}
}
